WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.330
Film Courage: You’ve talked [previously]
about when you attend Tisch for Film and Television,

00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:08.320
what was the curriculum regarding writing
at that time?

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:11.460
If you were to take [those classes] today
and teach there today, what would you add?

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:12.460
What would you change?

00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:14.180
What would you keep the same?

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:19.000
Daniel Calvisi: Well, there wasn’t a ton
of screenwriting when I was there.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:25.289
My favorite class was Screenplay Analysis,
no shocker there.

00:00:25.289 --> 00:00:28.699
The first produced screenplay that I read
was in that class.

00:00:28.699 --> 00:00:31.689
We read the SILENCE OF THE LAMBS which was
a really great script.

00:00:31.689 --> 00:00:37.930
It was interesting because that script was
exactly 120 pages and they always say one

00:00:37.930 --> 00:00:44.360
page equals one minute so it was exactly 120
minutes (2 hours) and that got me thinking

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:51.770
Hmm, how would this writer know that he was
going to end exactly two hours in (120 pages

00:00:51.770 --> 00:00:53.320
in)?

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:57.520
And then in the course of the class we learned
about structure and how important structure

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:01.510
was and that was structured exactly to end
on page 120.

00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:04.390
Film Courage: You said there weren’t too
many classes on writing there?

00:01:04.390 --> 00:01:06.499
Of course the ones that were there, you loved.

00:01:06.499 --> 00:01:07.820
Dan: At that time, yes.

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:17.729
Film Courage: How would you expand on 
it today?

00:01:17.729 --> 00:01:18.799
What would you add to the curriculum?

00:01:18.799 --> 00:01:22.159
Let’s say they invited you to come back
as a teacher or professor?

00:01:22.159 --> 00:01:29.649
Dan: Well, I think I would definitely emphasize
television, the long form scripted television

00:01:29.649 --> 00:01:32.359
writing form.

00:01:32.359 --> 00:01:37.641
And I would probably emphasize low-budget
filmmaking, writing something that could be

00:01:37.641 --> 00:01:39.100
shot on a low-budget.

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:44.600
Now films are being shot on iPhones and they’re
getting released in theaters and they’re

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:47.600
getting nominated for awards and winning awards.

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:52.460
So I would go in and tell these kids they
probably already know it but you can shoot

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:57.570
a movie on anything, on any topic, on any
type of equipment and if it’s good and has

00:01:57.570 --> 00:02:04.030
a great story, it could have an afterlife
but it still has to have that great story.

00:02:04.030 --> 00:02:07.569
So it doesn’t matter what technology you
use.

00:02:07.569 --> 00:02:12.090
I would say learn about screenwriting and
learn about structure and focus on the writing

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:13.090
first.

00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:19.340
Film Courage: In terms of the actual breakdown
of writing a screenplay, what do you think

00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:23.700
you focus on more, structure or dialogue?

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:24.750
Dan: For students?

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:32.140
Film Courage: Yes, if you were to come back
as a professor and have say with the curriculum.

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:36.390
Dan: I would most definitely focus on structure
most.

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:40.959
I think just flowing, short, tight storytelling.

00:02:40.959 --> 00:02:47.370
There is nothing better than a two-page scene
that really advances the story and surprises

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:54.060
us and brings up and turns direction and shows
us an element of the character that we didn’t

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:55.060
see coming.

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:58.469
It’s all about surprise and subverting our
expectations.

00:02:58.469 --> 00:03:04.060
That’s why it’s so important not to have
clichés because the audience (the reader)

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:08.860
doesn’t want to know what’s going to happen
because it’s compelling if they’re surprised.

00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:14.569
So I would probably emphasize that, structure
and surprising storytelling, not cliché storytelling.

00:03:14.569 --> 00:03:20.069
Film Courage: So you went into the program
thinking about the industry and knowing there

00:03:20.069 --> 00:03:25.880
were certain films that you wanted to write
in a similar genre and voice.

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:30.010
When you came out of the program, what did
the real world show you?

00:03:30.010 --> 00:03:31.010
What was an eye-opener?

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:32.269
What was a disappointment?

00:03:32.269 --> 00:03:38.120
What was more of an unplanned joyous discovery
about the industry?

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:46.670
Dan: Well, it took me awhile to realize that
working in the industry at a day job, you

00:03:46.670 --> 00:03:48.280
don’t have to be creative.

00:03:48.280 --> 00:03:52.670
They don’t want you to be creative unless
you’re in a creative position.

00:03:52.670 --> 00:03:59.810
So I was so obsessed with selling a screenplay
and directing my first film when I was in

00:03:59.810 --> 00:04:08.099
my early 20’s and I was doing my first day
jobs as interns, as administrative assistant,

00:04:08.099 --> 00:04:16.019
as a reader and I was always looking for a
way to impress the boss that they would eventually

00:04:16.019 --> 00:04:18.229
bankroll my film or buy my script.

00:04:18.229 --> 00:04:22.830
What I didn’t realize was it was all about
working hard, put your head down, work harder

00:04:22.830 --> 00:04:28.180
than the next guy and then if you work hard
enough and they believe in you because you’ve

00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:33.550
worked so hard and shown that initiative and
taken any job out there no matter how (any

00:04:33.550 --> 00:04:40.729
task they give you no matter how bad or ugly
or gross or whatever take that job do the

00:04:40.729 --> 00:04:45.949
best job you can at it do it 120 percent and
that’s what’s going to get you hired,

00:04:45.949 --> 00:04:49.990
that’s what’s going to get you the better
position to move up the ladder.

00:04:49.990 --> 00:04:54.319
Film Courage: When you say that they don’t
necessarily want people who are creative,

00:04:54.319 --> 00:04:55.319
that’s interesting.

00:04:55.319 --> 00:05:00.180
So even if you’re a fantastic writer it
doesn’t mean you’re going to get a certain

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:04.880
job where writing skills aren’t at the forefront
of what you’re doing?

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:12.580
Dan: Yeah, like if you’re working in development
your job is to evaluate material and develop

00:05:12.580 --> 00:05:16.820
material for your employer’s company.

00:05:16.820 --> 00:05:25.789
It’s not to write your own script, it’s
to find material that’s right for your employer

00:05:25.789 --> 00:05:30.810
and then development that material so it can
be a greenlit film.

00:05:30.810 --> 00:05:35.139
So if you’re a screenwriter working in development
let’s say and your an assistant to a creative

00:05:35.139 --> 00:05:42.860
director or director of development at a studio,
they don’t want you to be writing your screenplay

00:05:42.860 --> 00:05:47.240
two hours of the day, they want you to support
them in finding great material elsewhere.

00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:53.250
Film Courage: Do you think internships are
good for someone coming out of film school?

00:05:53.250 --> 00:05:55.080
Dan: Yes, I definitely think so.

00:05:55.080 --> 00:06:01.000
It can be challenging because how do you pay
your rent if you’re working at a free internship?

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:03.370
And that’s one challenge that I had, as
well.

00:06:03.370 --> 00:06:04.490
I used to do temp work.

00:06:04.490 --> 00:06:09.800
I used to do administrative assistant temp
work at the time and try to do internships

00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:13.060
at the same time which was challenging.

00:06:13.060 --> 00:06:18.889
But definitely internships the main advice
is just work hard, work harder than the next

00:06:18.889 --> 00:06:19.889
guy.

00:06:19.889 --> 00:06:22.520
Film Courage: That sounds like a lot, how
did you juggle that?

00:06:22.520 --> 00:06:25.580
Dan: Well it was tough.

00:06:25.580 --> 00:06:29.970
I would try to get temp jobs, I would tell
my supervisors at the temp company that I

00:06:29.970 --> 00:06:35.080
wanted jobs that were only like one, two or
three days and then hopefully that would make

00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:39.919
me enough money maybe for a couple weeks and
then those two or three days that I wasn’t

00:06:39.919 --> 00:06:45.090
working that week I would go into the internship
at the film company.

00:06:45.090 --> 00:06:47.069
It was tough but I figured it out somehow.

